Unit-0.5 core + CLI evidence (synthetic data only)
Toolchain: rustc 1.90.0 (1159e78c4 2025-09-14); cargo 1.90.0.
Branch: feat/unit-0.5-backup. No commit, network, sudo, or third-party dependency added.

Tests first: core/tests/unit05_backup.rs, unit05_faults.rs, CLI test were written before implementation.
Initial core test run failed to compile because backup module / Store backup APIs did not yet exist.
Additional regressions were first reproduced for identical state commit detection and non-JSON leading-zero integers.
Existing test files / assertions are unchanged.

Public APIs:
Store::export_backup(&Path, &str) -> Result<ExportReport, CoreError>
backup::verify_backup(&Path) -> Result<BackupInfo, CoreError>
Store::restore_backup_overwrite(&mut self, &Path) -> Result<RestoreReport, CoreError>
Types (backup module and crate reexports): BackupCounts, MissingMedia, BackupInfo, ExportReport, RestoreReport; each has to_json().
sha256::Sha256::{new, update, finalize}, Default (streaming; existing digest APIs retained).
CLI: export-backup <dest>; verify-backup <file>; restore-backup <file> --yes.
verify-backup does not open/create a Store; missing --yes is rejected before opening/creating a Store.

Format:
8-byte SGBAK\0\0\x01; u64 LE manifest length; UTF-8 camelCase JSON; raw entries;
48-byte trailer = SGBAKEND + SHA-256(all preceding bytes) + u64 LE preceding length.
No compression/encryption; manifest cap 8 MiB; entry cap 100000; sizes bounded by actual package length.
State entry first, referenced media sorted by filename; missing/corrupt referenced originals omitted and reported once per asset.
Formal/draft counts count active rows with the corresponding snapshot; trashed counts count rows in trash.
ExportReport.sha256 hashes the COMPLETE package, including trailer (distinct from trailer digest).
Manifest and domain state JSON are materialized for parsing; container, media and state commit copy use 64 KiB buffers.

Deliberate implementation refinements / deviations:
- Reject Windows reserved device names, trailing dots, and case-insensitive path duplicates on every platform, in addition to SPEC whitelist.
- Backup JSON parsing rejects >128 nesting and leading-zero integers before the shared strict parser.
- Export publication uses same-directory hard_link + unlink instead of an overwriting rename: atomic no-clobber publication even if another exporter creates dest concurrently.
  Filesystems without hard-link support fail safely and leave no destination/partial; internal app data/cache filesystems are the intended path.
- Journal additionally stores stateSha256; delete journal LAST after archiving old and removing staging, so crashes during cleanup remain recoverable.
- If staged state bytes exactly equal current state bytes, append one legal JSON newline to staged state before switching. This distinguishes the commit point without changing records/markers/conflicts/seq/seed.
- Safety copies are permanent; replaced old media goes under safety/replaced/ and is never hard-deleted.
- Windows atomic state replacement uses MoveFileExW(REPLACE_EXISTING | WRITE_THROUGH), with temp handles closed first.
- Existing preview.rs modulo expression changed to is_multiple_of to resolve its pre-existing Clippy warning; behavior unchanged.

Scope / limits:
Sections 1-7 implemented. Section 8 (Tauri/TS/UI), merge, encryption and cross-backup anti-resurrection are not implemented by design.
Windows MSVC and Android cross-compilation checked offline; runtime tests executed on Linux only. macOS toolchain is not installed.
Unrelated concurrent src-tauri/Cargo.toml and src-tauri/Cargo.lock changes were observed and left untouched.

Safety cleanup ownership: only a directory successfully created by the current safety-copy attempt can be removed on failure; existing safety directories are preserved. Restore path allocation skips old/staging/safety name collisions.

